1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,480
 So now let's talk about cloud computing.

2
00:00:02,480 --> 00:00:04,040
 So what is cloud computing?

3
00:00:04,040 --> 00:00:06,120
 The definition is as such.

4
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:10,000
 Cloud computing is the on-demand delivery of compute power,

5
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,600
 database storage, application, and other IT resources.

6
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:17,280
 The very important keyword here is on-demand.

7
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:19,400
 You get it when you need it.

8
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:22,200
 And then through a cloud service platform,

9
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:25,400
 you're going to get a pay as you go pricing.

10
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:27,160
 That means that you're only going to pay

11
00:00:27,160 --> 00:00:29,840
 for what you requested when you requested it

12
00:00:29,840 --> 00:00:32,720
 and as you're using it, when you're done using it,

13
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:34,360
 you're not going to pay anymore.

14
00:00:34,360 --> 00:00:35,960
 This is a big shift, right?

15
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:37,760
 Then this is cloud computing.

16
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:41,560
 So we can provision exactly the right type and size

17
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:43,520
 of computing resources you need.

18
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:44,640
 Do you need a big server?

19
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:45,760
 We have that for you.

20
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:46,840
 Do you want a small one?

21
00:00:46,840 --> 00:00:47,880
 We have that too.

22
00:00:47,880 --> 00:00:48,640
 Do you want 10?

23
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:50,000
 Yes, do you want two tomorrow?

24
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,320
 Of course, the cloud really allows you to adapt

25
00:00:53,320 --> 00:00:55,680
 to the type and size you need.

26
00:00:55,680 --> 00:00:58,760
 Then you can access all these resources,

27
00:00:58,760 --> 00:01:01,680
 not with 24 hour notice, not with two hours notice,

28
00:01:01,680 --> 00:01:04,960
 but instantly you don't need to order things in advance.

29
00:01:04,960 --> 00:01:07,280
 When you want a server and you'll see this in this course,

30
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:09,360
 you'll have it within seconds.

31
00:01:09,360 --> 00:01:12,800
 Then the cloud will also give you a really nice interface.

32
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:16,000
 So you can easily access your servers, your storage,

33
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:19,680
 databases, and the set of application services.

34
00:01:19,680 --> 00:01:23,160
 Something about the cloud, but in specific AWS,

35
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:26,080
 which is Amazon Web Services, owns and maintains

36
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:29,520
 network connected hardware required for these application services,

37
00:01:29,520 --> 00:01:33,520
 while you provision and use what you need via a web application.

38
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:35,400
 So we'll see with this interface,

39
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:38,280
 we'll make all these things a reality.

40
00:01:38,280 --> 00:01:40,720
 Now, let's go back to our traditional IT.

41
00:01:40,720 --> 00:01:41,960
 So we're changing.

42
00:01:41,960 --> 00:01:44,200
 We have our office or our garage,

43
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:46,880
 but now instead of building our own data center,

44
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:49,200
 we're going to use the cloud and in the cloud,

45
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:52,440
 which is also data center is just not our data center.

46
00:01:52,440 --> 00:01:56,720
 We're going to have servers, one, two, three, as we need,

47
00:01:56,720 --> 00:01:58,840
 and as we go, and we'll just going to pay

48
00:01:58,840 --> 00:02:00,880
 for exactly what we're using.

49
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:03,320
 So you have actually been using the cloud

50
00:02:03,320 --> 00:02:06,240
 without even knowing it, because it is omnipresent,

51
00:02:06,240 --> 00:02:08,240
 but not necessarily visible.

52
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:11,920
 So if you use a web client such as Gmail, well, for example,

53
00:02:11,920 --> 00:02:13,920
 it's an email cloud service,

54
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:16,840
 and you're going to pay only for the emails you're stored.

55
00:02:16,840 --> 00:02:19,040
 You're not provisioning servers when you use Gmail.

56
00:02:19,040 --> 00:02:20,560
 You just use it.

57
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:22,680
 Maybe you've stored some data on the cloud.

58
00:02:22,680 --> 00:02:26,240
 Maybe through Dropbox, Google Drive, Google Photos, iCloud.

59
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:28,720
 I don't know, but with Dropbox, for example,

60
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:30,040
 it's a cloud storage service.

61
00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:32,080
 You're going to put your files on Dropbox,

62
00:02:32,080 --> 00:02:36,400
 and originally, fun fact, Dropbox was built on AWS.

63
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:38,880
 So we've been using a cloud storage service as well

64
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:42,080
 without knowing it, and Netflix, it's huge.

65
00:02:42,080 --> 00:02:45,040
 It is built entirely on AWS,

66
00:02:45,040 --> 00:02:46,720
 and it provides you a cloud service,

67
00:02:46,720 --> 00:02:49,400
 which is to get video on demand.

68
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:51,520
 Now, obviously, these cloud services

69
00:02:51,520 --> 00:02:53,160
 are very different from AWS,

70
00:02:53,160 --> 00:02:56,200
 but we'll learn what it goes behind these services

71
00:02:56,200 --> 00:03:00,720
 and how AWS can help you build these kind of cloud services.

72
00:03:00,720 --> 00:03:02,880
 So let's go one step further.

73
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:04,920
 They are different kinds of clouds out there.

74
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:07,240
 The first one is called a private cloud,

75
00:03:07,240 --> 00:03:10,640
 and a provider is could be rack space.

76
00:03:10,640 --> 00:03:13,760
 This is cloud services used by a single organization

77
00:03:13,760 --> 00:03:15,120
 they're not exposed to the public.

78
00:03:15,120 --> 00:03:16,920
 So you get your own private cloud,

79
00:03:16,920 --> 00:03:19,600
 your own private data center is just managed by someone else.

80
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:21,360
 You still have complete control over it,

81
00:03:21,360 --> 00:03:24,320
 and you have more security for sensitive application,

82
00:03:24,320 --> 00:03:27,160
 which may meet some specific business needs.

83
00:03:27,160 --> 00:03:29,280
 This is out of scope for this course,

84
00:03:29,280 --> 00:03:30,840
 but still good to mention.

85
00:03:30,840 --> 00:03:33,160
 Now, the public cloud is more interesting.

86
00:03:33,160 --> 00:03:36,120
 So three famous cloud providers that are public

87
00:03:36,120 --> 00:03:38,960
 are Microsoft Azure, Google Cloud,

88
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:41,400
 and Amazon Web Services,

89
00:03:41,400 --> 00:03:43,960
 that will be learning in this course, obviously.

90
00:03:43,960 --> 00:03:48,080
 So in this case, the cloud resources own and operated

91
00:03:48,080 --> 00:03:50,720
 by a third party cloud service provider

92
00:03:50,720 --> 00:03:53,360
 and they're delivered over the internet,

93
00:03:53,360 --> 00:03:57,040
 and we'll see the six advantages of using cloud computing.

94
00:03:57,040 --> 00:03:59,600
 So in this instance, that means that from AWS,

95
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:02,440
 we'll be able to request what we need when we want it.

96
00:04:02,440 --> 00:04:05,400
 And then lastly, which is also important for the exam,

97
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:07,840
 is the concept of a hybrid cloud.

98
00:04:07,840 --> 00:04:09,800
 So with hybrid, we're actually going to do a mix

99
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:10,960
 of private and public.

100
00:04:10,960 --> 00:04:13,720
 We're going to keep some servers on premises,

101
00:04:13,720 --> 00:04:16,520
 and we'll extend some of the capabilities we need

102
00:04:16,520 --> 00:04:17,600
 into the cloud.

103
00:04:17,600 --> 00:04:21,360
 That means that we'll have a hybrid of our own infrastructure

104
00:04:21,360 --> 00:04:23,440
 and the AWS cloud.

105
00:04:23,440 --> 00:04:25,440
 We'll have control over sensitive assets

106
00:04:25,440 --> 00:04:27,240
 in your private infrastructure,

107
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:30,480
 but we'll have the flexibility and the cost effectiveness

108
00:04:30,480 --> 00:04:32,480
 of using the public cloud.

109
00:04:32,480 --> 00:04:36,800
 Now, five characteristics of cloud computing.

110
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:41,400
 The first one is that it's fully on demand and self-service.

111
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:43,400
 Users, and we'll see this in this course,

112
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:45,160
 we will be able to provision resources

113
00:04:45,160 --> 00:04:47,240
 and use them without having anyone

114
00:04:47,240 --> 00:04:49,720
 from AWS intervene.

115
00:04:49,720 --> 00:04:52,840
 Then we'll be having access to broad network.

116
00:04:52,840 --> 00:04:55,200
 The resources will be available over the network,

117
00:04:55,200 --> 00:04:57,400
 and they can be accessed in diverse ways,

118
00:04:57,400 --> 00:04:59,000
 as we'll see in this course.

119
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:01,040
 It'll be multi-tenancy and we'll have resource pooling.

120
00:05:01,040 --> 00:05:02,920
 So that means that's not just us,

121
00:05:02,920 --> 00:05:07,320
 but other customers from AWS can share the same infrastructure

122
00:05:07,320 --> 00:05:11,720
 and applications while still having security and privacy.

123
00:05:11,720 --> 00:05:14,240
 And then these multiple customers are going service

124
00:05:14,240 --> 00:05:16,280
 from the same physical resources.

125
00:05:16,280 --> 00:05:19,200
 So here, me, you and other customers,

126
00:05:19,200 --> 00:05:22,920
 we're going to share this entire data center of the cloud.

127
00:05:22,920 --> 00:05:26,360
 This gives us rapid elasticity and scalability.

128
00:05:26,360 --> 00:05:29,600
 That means that we can automatically and quickly acquire

129
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:32,240
 and dispose resources when we need.

130
00:05:32,240 --> 00:05:35,680
 And that means that we can quickly and easily scale

131
00:05:35,680 --> 00:05:36,760
 based on demand.

132
00:05:36,760 --> 00:05:39,880
 And that is a major advantage of the cloud.

133
00:05:39,880 --> 00:05:41,680
 Finally, it's a measured service.

134
00:05:41,680 --> 00:05:43,440
 So the usage is going to be measured

135
00:05:43,440 --> 00:05:46,240
 and we're going to pay exactly for what we have used.

136
00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:49,040
 This is a big shift from on-premises.

137
00:05:49,040 --> 00:05:51,080
 Now, six advantages.

138
00:05:51,080 --> 00:05:53,400
 We are going to trade capital expenses

139
00:05:53,400 --> 00:05:55,120
 far or operational expenses.

140
00:05:55,120 --> 00:05:56,600
 So CapEx for UpEx.

141
00:05:56,600 --> 00:05:58,920
 That means that you don't own hardware,

142
00:05:58,920 --> 00:06:00,640
 you're going to pay on demand

143
00:06:00,640 --> 00:06:03,080
 and that will reduce your total cost of ownership,

144
00:06:03,080 --> 00:06:06,520
 your TCO and your operational expense.

145
00:06:06,520 --> 00:06:08,720
 That means that you don't buy it hardware in advance.

146
00:06:08,720 --> 00:06:11,600
 You're just going to rent it from AWS.

147
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:15,280
 Then we're going to benefit from massive economies of scale.

148
00:06:15,280 --> 00:06:18,880
 The price is because we are using AWS, not just us,

149
00:06:18,880 --> 00:06:21,320
 but other customers and so many people are using it,

150
00:06:21,320 --> 00:06:23,840
 then the prices will be reduced by AWS over time

151
00:06:23,840 --> 00:06:26,960
 because AWS will be more efficient at running

152
00:06:26,960 --> 00:06:29,040
 due to its large scale.

153
00:06:29,040 --> 00:06:31,240
 We also need to stop guessing capacity.

154
00:06:31,240 --> 00:06:34,000
 Before, we had to plan and buy servers in advance

155
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:36,360
 and hope that it would meet the capacity.

156
00:06:36,360 --> 00:06:39,240
 But now, we can actually scale automatically

157
00:06:39,240 --> 00:06:42,960
 based on the actual measured usage for our application.

158
00:06:42,960 --> 00:06:44,560
 And because everything is on demand,

159
00:06:44,560 --> 00:06:46,640
 we have increased speed and agility.

160
00:06:46,640 --> 00:06:49,720
 We can create, operate and do stuff right away.

161
00:06:49,720 --> 00:06:52,400
 No blockers for us to be efficient.

162
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:54,920
 And finally, we have a huge cost

163
00:06:54,920 --> 00:06:56,320
 that we don't need to have anymore,

164
00:06:56,320 --> 00:06:58,840
 which is we can stop spending money running

165
00:06:58,840 --> 00:07:00,440
 and maintaining data centers.

166
00:07:00,440 --> 00:07:03,200
 And this allows a team of, say, five people

167
00:07:03,200 --> 00:07:06,120
 to create a global application in minutes.

168
00:07:06,120 --> 00:07:09,720
 Thanks to leveraging this AWS global infrastructure

169
00:07:09,720 --> 00:07:11,920
 that is going to be worldwide.

170
00:07:11,920 --> 00:07:14,400
 Okay, so the problems we just solved by using cloud

171
00:07:14,400 --> 00:07:18,000
 is that we're more flexible, we're more cost effective.

172
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:21,160
 We are more scalable because we can add resources

173
00:07:21,160 --> 00:07:22,680
 as we need to go along.

174
00:07:22,680 --> 00:07:25,400
 We're elastic, we can scale out and scale in when needed.

175
00:07:25,400 --> 00:07:28,600
 We also have high availability and fault tolerance

176
00:07:28,600 --> 00:07:30,640
 because we don't rely on the one data center.

177
00:07:30,640 --> 00:07:34,040
 We rely on the fleet of data centers all around the world.

178
00:07:34,040 --> 00:07:36,240
 We're more agile, we can rapidly develop,

179
00:07:36,240 --> 00:07:38,280
 test and launch software applications.

180
00:07:38,280 --> 00:07:42,840
 And although this make the cloud a really no brainer.

181
00:07:42,840 --> 00:07:44,520
 So that's it just for an introduction

182
00:07:44,520 --> 00:07:46,560
 of how the cloud is going to be effective.

183
00:07:46,560 --> 00:07:48,760
 Now in the next lecture, we're going to view

184
00:07:48,760 --> 00:07:51,080
 one step further, whether the different types

185
00:07:51,080 --> 00:07:52,080
 of cloud computing.

